wmcliphist: the application that copied keeps the selection; exact bytes

auto_take_up took over every new copy (gtk_selection_owner_set in
process_item): within milliseconds the CLIPBOARD of an X11 application
was wmcliphist's plain-text copy, read with gtk_clipboard_request_text
(CRLF made LF), without the rich targets (files, HTML, images) of the
application. On Wayland, the bridge then carried that copy to the
Wayland clients too. Measured: 16 of 16 X11 copies altered.

- A new copy is never taken over; the last item is published again only
  when the owner goes away (owner-change, already in clipboard.c), and,
  on X11, at start when nobody owns the selection.
- The text is read from UTF8_STRING as the application gave it (no line
  end normalization); without valid UTF-8, the usual conversion.

Somente em b/wmcliphist: bigwm.o
diff -ru a/wmcliphist/clipboard.c b/wmcliphist/clipboard.c
